  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A two-story wave of warm molasses swallowed an entire city neighborhood. In 1919, a steel tank in Boston exploded, unleashing two million gallons of sludge.",
        "visual_query": "1919 boston street historic buildings",
        "visual_fallback": "historic city street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The thirty-five-mile-per-hour wave crushed buildings and buckled elevated railway tracks. The company immediately blamed anarchist explosives.",
        "visual_query": "1910s industrial city destruction damage",
        "visual_fallback": "damaged industrial building"
      },
      {
        "narration": "But there was no bomb\u2014it was fatal physics. Unseasonable warmth caused fermenting syrup to expand, building massive gas pressure inside.",
        "visual_query": "vintage industrial steel storage tank",
        "visual_fallback": "industrial steel tank"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Modern analysis proved the steel walls were half as thick as safety codes required and cracked easily in winter cold.",
        "visual_query": "cracked steel metal industrial structure",
        "visual_fallback": "metal fatigue texture"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The owners had even painted the tank brown to hide visible leaks. If executives knew the structure was failing, why did they fill it?",
        "visual_query": "1910s boston harbor industrial district",
        "visual_fallback": "historic industrial harbor"
      }
